# Pitfalls — SOLID Principles

## ❌ Pitfall 1: Over-Engineering with SOLID

SOLID is not a goal in itself. Applying every principle to a 50-line utility class creates:
- Excessive abstraction layers
- Interface explosion (dozens of one-method interfaces)
- Indirection that makes simple code hard to read

> **Rule**: Apply SOLID when you feel the *pain* (hard to test, hard to extend, hard to reason about) — not upfront as a ritual.

## ❌ Pitfall 2: SRP Confusion — Classes vs Functions

SRP applies to *reasons to change*, not to *number of methods*:

```cpp
// ❌ Misapplied SRP: splitting by method count, not by reason to change
class UserFirstName  { std::string get(); void set(std::string); };
class UserLastName   { std::string get(); void set(std::string); };
class UserEmail      { std::string get(); void set(std::string); };
// ← These all change for the same reason: User domain model changes

// ✅ User fields change together → one class
struct User { std::string firstName, lastName, email; };
```

## ❌ Pitfall 3: LSP Violation — Throwing in Overrides

An override that throws where the base doesn't violates LSP:

```cpp
struct Stream {
    virtual void write(std::string_view data) {}  // base: no-throw acceptable
};

struct ReadOnlyStream : Stream {
    void write(std::string_view) override {
        throw std::logic_error("read-only!");  // ❌ caller expects write to work
    }
};
```

If a subtype can't honor the base contract, it shouldn't inherit.

## ❌ Pitfall 4: DIP ≠ Always Use Interfaces

Dependency inversion doesn't mean every dependency needs a virtual interface:

```cpp
// ❌ Unnecessary abstraction for a simple math helper
struct IMathHelper { virtual double sqrt(double) = 0; };
class Processor { IMathHelper& math_; ... };

// ✅ Just use it directly (or pass as a template/function if needed)
class Processor { void run(double x) { auto r = std::sqrt(x); ... } };
```

## Prevention Checklist

- Apply principles to ease *real* pain, not hypothetical future requirements
- The "one reason to change" test: describe the class in one sentence without "and"
- Check LSP: can you replace the base with any subtype in all existing tests without failure?
- Prefer constructor injection — makes dependencies visible and testable
- Use ISP to avoid forcing mock/stub code to implement unused methods

